(window.IBALL_PERIODICAL_LIBRARY = window.IBALL_PERIODICAL_LIBRARY || {})["2026-09-17"] = {"meta":{"id":"2026-09-17","label":"2026 年 9 月 17 日","date":"2026-09-17","range":"","theme":"科技","themes":["科技"],"sources":["Economist"],"title":"No more excuses","titleZh":"别再找借口了","counts":{"article":0,"paragraph":9,"vocab":29,"question":20,"qa":0,"original":5,"layout":1},"tabs":{"articles":false,"reading":true,"tests":true,"qa":false,"originals":true}},"articles":[],"reading":[{"file":"9.17 精读(1).pdf","headline":{"title":"No more excuses","titleZh":"别再找借口了","source":"Economist","series":"考研那些事儿之科技系列"},"paragraphs":[{"index":1,"en":"Schools in RICH countries are failing. The latest scores on PISA tests of reading, maths and science, released on September 8th, are the worst since the tests, administered by the OECD, began in 2000. Educators can no longer blame it all on covid-19 lockdowns, which badly messed up lessons and caused PISA grades to drop. The pandemic is now over, but scores have continued to fall roughly as fast. In 35 rich countries that take part in the tests, a typical 15-year-old now reads no better than a 14-year-old did a decade ago. A slow-motion disaster is under way.","zh":"富裕国家的学校正在走向失败。9月8日公布的最新一轮PISA（国际学生评估项目）阅读、数学与科学测试成绩，创下自2000年该项考试（由经合组织OECD负责实施）开考以来的最差纪录。教育工作者再也无法把这一切都归咎于新冠封锁——它曾严重扰乱课堂教学，导致PISA成绩下滑。如今疫情早已结束，但分数仍在以大致相同的速度继续下跌。在参加测试的35个富裕国家中，如今一名普通15岁学生的阅读水平还比不上10年前14岁学生的水平。一场“慢动作式”的灾难正在上演。"},{"index":2,"en":"Test scores are not the full measure of a child. But they are a good predictor of desirable outcomes and a bulwark against many of the worst ones. Kids who do well on tests tend to earn more, live longer and healthier lives, and commit fewer crimes. By one estimate, pushing up PISA scores by a mere quarter of a standard deviation might increase annual growth in rich countries by half a percentage point. (r)/ a sign or fact that shows what","zh":"考试成绩并非衡量一个孩子的全部标准。但它们却是预判未来良性发展的良好指标，也是抵御诸多糟糕人生际遇的坚实屏障。考试表现优异的孩子往往收入更高、活得更久更健康，犯罪率也更低。据一项估算，只要把PISA成绩提高区区四分之一个标准差，就能让富裕国家的年度经济增长率提升半个百分点。"},{"index":3,"en":"Perhaps, though, as artificial intelligence improves, the fact that children struggle intellectually will not matter so much? The adults of the future will be able to outsource their thinking to clever machines, just as they use machines to perform innumerable physical tasks. So why sweat about swotting?","zh":"不过，也许随着人工智能不断进步，孩子们学习吃力这件事,也就不那么重要了？未来的成年人将能够把思考\"外包\"给智能的机器，正如他们借助机器来完成数不清的体力劳动一样。那么，何必还要为埋头苦读伤脑筋呢？"},{"index":4,"en":"This argument could not be more wrong. AI will not make it pointless to train human brains, any more than the invention of cars eliminated the need for physical exercise. On the contrary, adapting to a world of rapid, AI-driven change will require more mental agility, not less. And precisely because AI will increase the temptation for people to outsource difficult cognitive tasks, schools have an extra duty to help pupils exercise their brains and acquire the hard habits of thinking. Failure to furnish them with the basic building blocks of a welldeveloped mind, such as literacy and numeracy, is inexcusable.","zh":"这种论点错得离谱。AI并不会让训练人类大脑变得毫无意义，正如汽车的发明并没有消除锻炼身体的需要一样。恰恰相反，要适应一个人工智能驱动的快速变化的世界，我们需要的是更高的思维敏捷度，而非更低。而且，正因为AI会加剧人们把复杂认知任务\"外包\"出去的诱惑，学校更有责任帮助学生锻炼大脑、养成刻苦钻研的习惯。若不能为孩子们提供健全心智的基石——比如读写能力和计算能力——那是不可原谅的。"},{"index":5,"en":"PISA scores began drifting downwards around 2012. The evidence is growing that tablets, mobile phones and other such screens are distracting youngsters from their studies and diverting them from hobbies, like reading books, that instil the ability to focus. So limits on screens seem prudent. About half of countries now ban mobile phones from schools to some extent; more should follow. Used carefully, technology can benefit classrooms. More usually, snazzy new tools and devices just get in the way.","zh":"PISA成绩大约从2012年开始逐渐下滑。越来越多的证据表明，平板电脑、手机和其他此类屏幕正在分散青少年对学业的注意力，使他们远离阅读书籍等能培养专注能力的爱好。因此，限制屏幕使用时间似乎是明智之举。目前大约有一半的国家在一定程度上禁止在学校使用手机；更多国家应该效仿。若使用得当，技术能让课堂受益。但更常见的情况是，花哨的新工具和新设备只会带来妨碍。"},{"index":6,"en":"is muddle-headed thinking among educators. Some have fallen for the leftist canard that tests are harmful and grades are racist. Some have allowed reasonable worries about children’s mental health to warp into an excuse for low expectations. Some unhelpful habits acquired during the pandemic have stuck: children are still missing more lessons than before, and many teachers who relaxed their grading standards have failed to tighten up","zh":"然而，更大的问题在于教育工作者自身思路混乱。一些人听信了左翼的谬论，认为考试有害、分数带有种族主义色彩。一些人将对孩子心理健康的合理担忧，扭曲成了降低要求的借口。一些疫情期间养成的不良习惯仍然存在：学生缺课比以往更多，许多放宽了评分标准的教师也未能重新严格起来。"},{"index":7,"en":"What to do? There is little evidence that shovelling more cash into schools will make much difference: Japan spends 30% less per pupil than America and gets much better results. But research needs a big boost. Teaching, as a profession, remains shamefully uninterested in the evidence about which techniques work best. Education departments in universities contain some of the weakest and most radical staff, often keener on abstract ideas of social justice than the more concrete benefits that better grades might bring to pupils. large amounts of something","zh":"该怎么办？几乎没有证据表明向学校大量投入资金就能产生明显效果：日本每个学生的教育支出比美国低30%，却取得了好得多的成绩。但研究亟需大力加强。今人汗颜的是，教学这一行业仍对有关哪种教学方法最为有效的证据漠不关心。大学里的教育院系聚集着一些能力薄弱、理念激进的教职人员，他们往往更热衷于抽象的社会正义理念，而不是更好的成绩可能给学生带来的更具体的益处。"},{"index":8,"en":"Places that have avoided declines deserve more attention. Britain is one. In 2009 its pupils scraped into the world’s top 30 in maths and reading. Now they sit in the top ten. Some of this stems from a return in England, under a previous Conservative government, to old-fashioned things such as rigorous exams, tough inspections and fact-filled curriculums.","zh":"那些成绩没有出现下滑的地区值得更多关注。英国就是其中之一。2009年，英国学生的数学与阅读成绩勉强挤进世界前30名。如今他们位居前十。其中部分原因在于，英格兰在前保守党政府执政时期，重拾一系列传统举措，例如严格考试、严厉督导以及重视基础知识的课程体系。"},{"index":9,"en":"Falling scores will not be fixed in a jiffy. Because it takes time to educate a child, the latest test results bear the marks of decisions taken ten to 20 years ago. That long lag makes reform politically harder. Governments that make schools better will seldom see benefits they can boast about before the next election. But they should care anyway. Politicians in past decades, from Tony Blair to George Bush, have won office promising to fix education. Today’s leaders seem to have cooled on schools, except as culture-war battlegrounds. That lack of serious","zh":"解决成绩下滑的问题，无法一蹴而就。因为教育一个孩子需要时间，最新的考试结果折射出10～20年前各项决策留下的印记。这种漫长的滞后使得教育改革在政治层面局步维艰。改善学校教育的政府，很少能在下届大选前收获可供炫耀的政绩。但政府仍然应当予以重视。过去几十年的政客们，从托尼·布莱尔到乔治·布什，都曾以承诺改革教育而赢得大选。如今的领导人似乎对学校兴趣索然，除非是把学校当作文化战争的战场。这种缺乏认真关注的态度将招致灾祸。随着机器变得越来越聪明，切莫让青少年变得越来越迟钝。"}],"vocab":[{"term":"plunge","head":"v. to fall suddenly and quickly from a high level or position The stock market plunged by 8% in a single day.","definition":"to fall suddenly and quickly from a high level or position The stock market plunged by 8% in a single day.","gloss":"骤降，暴跌；纵身投入股市单日暴跌 8%。","example":"","exampleZh":"","tags":["考研大纲词汇"]},{"term":"administer","head":"v. to manage and organize the dˈmɪnɪst ə ə affairs of a company, an organization, etc.","definition":"to manage and organize the dˈmɪnɪst ə ə affairs of a company, an organization, etc.","gloss":"管理，执行；主持（考试）","example":"The test is administered by an independent agency.","exampleZh":"该考试由一家独立机构主持。","tags":["考研大纲词汇"]},{"term":"lockdown","head":"n. an official order to control the movement of people, especially in an emergency Millions of children fell behind during the pandemic lockdowns.","definition":"an official order to control the movement of people, especially in an emergency Millions of children fell behind during the pandemic lockdowns.","gloss":"封锁，禁闭（措施）疫情期间的封锁让数百万儿童学业落后。","example":"","exampleZh":"","tags":[]},{"term":"predictor","head":"n. /prɪˈdɪkt ə will happen in the future","definition":"/prɪˈdɪkt ə will happen in the future","gloss":"预测指标，预示因素 Early reading ability is a strong predictor of later academic success. 早期阅读能力是日后学业成功的有力预 测指标。 k/ a person or thing that protects or","example":"","exampleZh":"","tags":[]},{"term":"bulwark","head":"n. / ˈ bʊlw ə defends something","definition":"/ ˈ bʊlw ə defends something","gloss":"堡垒，屏障，保障 A good education is a bulwark against poverty. 良好的教 育是抵御贫困的屏障。","example":"","exampleZh":"","tags":[]},{"term":"deviation","head":"n. the act of moving away from what is normal or acceptable The scores varied widely, with a standard deviation of 15 points.","definition":"the act of moving away from what is normal or acceptable The scores varied widely, with a standard deviation of 15 points.","gloss":"偏离，偏差分数差异很大，标准差为 15 分。","example":"","exampleZh":"","tags":["考研大纲词汇"]},{"term":"outsource","head":"v. to arrange for work to be done by another company or person Many firms outsource their customer service to call centres abroad.","definition":"to arrange for work to be done by another company or person Many firms outsource their customer service to call centres abroad.","gloss":"外包，把……交给外部处理许多公司把客服外包给海外呼叫中心。","example":"","exampleZh":"","tags":[]},{"term":"sweat","head":"v. to be very anxious or worried about something; to produce sweat Don't sweat the small stuff — focus on what matters.","definition":"to be very anxious or worried about something; to produce sweat Don't sweat the small stuff — focus on what matters.","gloss":"焦虑，担心；出汗【考研大纲词汇】别为琐事焦虑，专注于要紧的事。","example":"","exampleZh":"","tags":["考研大纲 词汇"]},{"term":"swot","head":"v. (BrE, informal) to study very hard, /swɒt/ especially in order to prepare for an exam （ He's swotting for his final exams this week.","definition":"(BrE, informal) to study very hard, /swɒt/ especially in order to prepare for an exam （ He's swotting for his final exams this week.","gloss":"尤指为应考）刻苦学习，死用功他这周正在为期末考试拼命复习。","example":"","exampleZh":"","tags":[]},{"term":"agility","head":"n. the ability to move or think quickly and əˈdʒɪl ə easily Mental agility is increasingly valued in the modern workplace.","definition":"the ability to move or think quickly and əˈdʒɪl ə easily Mental agility is increasingly valued in the modern workplace.","gloss":"敏捷，灵活，机敏现代职场越来越看重思维敏捷。","example":"","exampleZh":"","tags":[]},{"term":"temptation","head":"n. the desire to do or have something, especially something wrong or unwise I resisted the temptation to check my phone during the lecture.","definition":"the desire to do or have something, especially something wrong or unwise I resisted the temptation to check my phone during the lecture.","gloss":"诱惑，引诱我克制住了上课期间看手机的冲动。","example":"","exampleZh":"","tags":["考研大纲词汇"]},{"term":"divert","head":"v. to make somebody/something change direction or purpose","definition":"to make somebody/something change direction or purpose","gloss":"转移，使转向 Social media diverts young people's attention away from their studies. 社交媒体转移了年轻人对学业的注意力。","example":"","exampleZh":"","tags":["考研大纲词汇","考研大纲词汇"]},{"term":"instil","head":"v. to gradually make somebody feel, think or behave in a particular way Parents should instil good habits in their children from an early age.","definition":"to gradually make somebody feel, think or behave in a particular way Parents should instil good habits in their children from an early age.","gloss":"逐渐灌输，培养（品质、能力）父母应该从小培养孩子的好习惯。","example":"","exampleZh":"","tags":[]},{"term":"prudent","head":"a. dj. sensible and careful when you make judgements and decisions","definition":"dj. sensible and careful when you make judgements and decisions","gloss":"谨慎的，明智的【考研大纲词汇】","example":"It would be prudent to save some money for emergencies.","exampleZh":"存些钱以备不时之需是明智的。","tags":["考研大 纲词汇"]},{"term":"snazzy","head":"a. dj. fashionable, bright and modern, and attracting your attention SYN jazzy, smart a snazzy tie","definition":"dj. fashionable, bright and modern, and attracting your attention SYN jazzy, smart a snazzy tie","gloss":"漂亮而时髦的；吸引人的漂亮的领带","example":"","exampleZh":"","tags":[]},{"term":"muddle-headed","head":"a. dj. /ˌmʌdl ˈhedɪd/ confused; not thinking clearly","definition":"dj. /ˌmʌdl ˈhedɪd/ confused; not thinking clearly","gloss":"头脑混乱的，糊涂的","example":"Muddle-headed policies have done more harm than good.","exampleZh":"头脑混乱的政策弊大于利。","tags":[]},{"term":"canard","head":"n. a false report or story that people spread deliberately The claim that exams harm children is a canard. \"","definition":"a false report or story that people spread deliberately The claim that exams harm children is a canard. \"","gloss":"谣言，虚假报道考试有害\"的说法纯属谣言。","example":"","exampleZh":"","tags":[]},{"term":"warp","head":"v. to become or make something become bent or twisted; to change behaviour in a negative way Years of criticism had warped her confidence.","definition":"to become or make something become bent or twisted; to change behaviour in a negative way Years of criticism had warped her confidence.","gloss":"使变形，使扭曲多年的批评扭曲了她的自信。","example":"","exampleZh":"","tags":["考研大纲词汇"]},{"term":"shovel","head":"v. to put somewhere quickly Shovelling more money at the problem will not solve it.","definition":"to put somewhere quickly Shovelling more money at the problem will not solve it.","gloss":"大量投入，大把地塞向这个问题大量砸钱并不能解决它。","example":"","exampleZh":"","tags":[]},{"term":"boost","head":"n. an increase in something; something that helps or encourages The tax cut gave a big boost to consumer spending.","definition":"an increase in something; something that helps or encourages The tax cut gave a big boost to consumer spending.","gloss":"推动，促进，提升减税大大推动了消费支出。","example":"","exampleZh":"","tags":["考研大纲词汇"]},{"term":"radical","head":"a. dj. in favour of complete social or political change; very new and different","definition":"dj. in favour of complete social or political change; very new and different","gloss":"激 进的 ， 彻 底的","example":"The university's education department is known for its radical views.","exampleZh":"这所大学的教育学院以观点激进著称。","tags":["考研大纲词汇"]},{"term":"scrape","head":"v. to manage to succeed or pass with great difficulty The team scraped into the finals by a single point. r s/ done carefully and with great","definition":"to manage to succeed or pass with great difficulty The team scraped into the finals by a single point. r s/ done carefully and with great","gloss":"勉强获得，艰难达到该队以一分之差勉强挤进决赛。","example":"","exampleZh":"","tags":["考研大纲词汇"]},{"term":"rigorous","head":"a. dj. /ˈrɪɡ ə ə attention to detail; strict","definition":"dj. /ˈrɪɡ ə ə attention to detail; strict","gloss":"严格的，严谨的 Rigorous testing is essential before a new drug can be approved. 新药获批前必须经过严格的测试。","example":"","exampleZh":"","tags":["考研大纲词汇","考研大纲词汇"]},{"term":"inspection","head":"n. an official visit to check that rules are being obeyed The school passed its annual inspection with flying colours.","definition":"an official visit to check that rules are being obeyed The school passed its annual inspection with flying colours.","gloss":"检查，视察，督导这所学校以优异成绩通过了年度督导检查。","example":"","exampleZh":"","tags":["考研大纲词汇"]},{"term":"curriculum","head":"n. the subjects that are taught in əˈrɪkj ə ə a school, college, etc.","definition":"the subjects that are taught in əˈrɪkj ə ə a school, college, etc.","gloss":"课程，全部课程","example":"The national curriculum includes compulsory maths and science.","exampleZh":"国家课程包含必修的数学和科学。","tags":["考研大纲词汇"]},{"term":"jiffy","head":"n. a very short time I'll be back in a jiffy.","definition":"a very short time I'll be back in a jiffy.","gloss":"一会儿，瞬间我马上就回来。","example":"","exampleZh":"","tags":[]},{"term":"lag","head":"n. a period of time between one event and another There is often a time lag between a policy decision and its results.","definition":"a period of time between one event and another There is often a time lag between a policy decision and its results.","gloss":"滞后，延迟政策决定与其效果之间往往存在时间滞后。","example":"","exampleZh":"","tags":["考研大纲词汇"]},{"term":"calamity","head":"n. a serious accident or disaster əˈlæm ə Ignoring the warning signs would invite calamity.","definition":"a serious accident or disaster əˈlæm ə Ignoring the warning signs would invite calamity.","gloss":"灾难，祸患忽 视警示信号将会招致灾难。","example":"","exampleZh":"","tags":["考研大纲词汇"]},{"term":"dim","head":"a. dj. not bright; (of a person) not intelligent","definition":"dj. not bright; (of a person) not intelligent","gloss":"昏暗的；愚钝的","example":"The future looks dim for those who drop out of school.","exampleZh":"辍学者前途黯淡。","tags":["考研大纲词汇"]}]}],"tests":[{"file":"【福利】9.17 检验题__阅读理解(1).docx","type":"reading","instructions":[],"passage":[],"items":[{"number":21,"stem":"According to Paragraphs 1 and 2, which of the following is true about PISA scores?","options":[{"key":"A","text":"They have continued to fall since the pandemic, and stronger results are associated with better adult outcomes"},{"key":"B","text":"They recovered quickly after covid-19 lockdowns ended because ordinary classroom teaching had finally returned"},{"key":"C","text":"They are described as a complete measure of a child but a weak predictor of later life"},{"key":"D","text":"They show that schools in rich countries have now completely eliminated the effects of disrupted lessons"}],"answer":"A","kicker":"[A]","analysis":["【题型】细节理解题·数据与后果正误判断","【难度】⭐⭐","【定位】Paragraphs 1-2","【解析】","- 原文（Para.1）：Educators can no longer blame it all on covid-19 lockdowns, which badly messed up lessons and caused PISA grades to drop. The pandemic is now over, but scores have continued to fall roughly as fast.","- 原文（Para.2）：But they are a good predictor of desirable outcomes and a bulwark against many of the worst ones. Kids who do well on tests tend to earn more, live longer and healthier lives, and commit fewer crimes.","分析：","- 选项A（正确）：第 1 段明确说疫情结束后成绩仍以大致相同速度下降；第 2 段指出成绩好的孩子倾向收入更高、更健康长寿、犯罪更少。","- 选项B（错误）：反义干扰陷阱。疫情结束后成绩没有回升，而是 continued to fall。","- 选项C（错误）：双重反转陷阱。原文开头说 Test scores are not the full measure，随后又说它们是 good predictor，C 项把两者都反向改写。","- 选项D（错误）：过度推理陷阱。原文说 educators can no longer blame it all on covid-19，并没有说影响已完全消除。","【命题技巧】","- 疫情前后对比：lockdowns 与 pandemic is now over 构成时间分界。","- 预测作用定位：good predictor 与后续三项结果形成证据链。"]},{"number":22,"stem":"The word “bulwark” (Paragraph 2) is closest in meaning to _____.","options":[{"key":"A","text":"a decorative symbol that celebrates academic progress"},{"key":"B","text":"a protective barrier against harmful outcomes"},{"key":"C","text":"a temporary standard used only during examinations"},{"key":"D","text":"a statistical anomaly that complicates measurement"}],"answer":"B","kicker":"[B]","analysis":["【题型】词义猜测题·名词语境理解","【难度】⭐⭐⭐","【定位】Paragraph 2","【解析】","- 原文（Para.2）：But they are a good predictor of desirable outcomes and a bulwark against many of the worst ones. Kids who do well on tests tend to earn more, live longer and healthier lives, and commit fewer crimes.","分析：","- 选项A（错误）：情感与功能错位陷阱。下文列举的是实际收益和风险防护，不是象征性装饰。","- 选项B（正确）：bulwark 后接 against many of the worst ones，而 worst ones 指更差的人生结果；成绩好能“抵御”犯罪等问题，故为防护屏障。","- 选项C（错误）：无中生有陷阱。原文没有限定它只在考试期间使用。","- 选项D（错误）：概念偷换陷阱。原文谈预测和保护作用，不是统计异常。","【命题技巧】","- 介词搭配线索：bulwark against 提示防御功能。","- 下文例证反推：收入、健康、犯罪三个结果说明其保护含义。"]},{"number":23,"stem":"Paragraph 4 serves to _____.","options":[{"key":"A","text":"concede that artificial intelligence will eventually make literacy and numeracy unnecessary"},{"key":"B","text":"introduce a competing argument about AI and then leave its practical implications unresolved"},{"key":"C","text":"reject AI complacency by arguing that rapid technological change requires stronger human thinking"},{"key":"D","text":"criticise the invention of cars for making physical exercise completely pointless"}],"answer":"C","kicker":"[C]","analysis":["【题型】篇章结构题·反驳功能","【难度】⭐⭐⭐⭐","【定位】Paragraph 4","【解析】","- 原文（Para.4）：This argument could not be more wrong. AI will not make it pointless to train human brains, any more than the invention of cars eliminated the need for physical exercise.","- 原文（Para.4）：On the contrary, adapting to a world of rapid, AI-driven change will require more mental agility, not less... schools have an extra duty to help pupils exercise their brains and acquire the hard habits of thinking.","分析：","- 选项A（错误）：反向观点陷阱。作者明确说 AI will not make it pointless to train human brains。","- 选项B（错误）：结构误判陷阱。该段先反驳上一段观点，随后清晰指出学校负有额外责任，并非悬而不决。","- 选项C（正确）：段首 This argument could not be more wrong 直接否定上一段的 AI 乐观论；On the contrary 进一步说明 AI 时代需要更多 mental agility。","- 选项D（错误）：例子功能偷换陷阱。汽车与锻炼是类比，用来证明 AI 不会免除脑力训练，不是批评汽车本身。","【命题技巧】","- 强反驳句定位：could not be more wrong 是段落功能的结构信号。","- 类比结构识别：cars/exercise 平行对应 AI/human thinking。"]},{"number":24,"stem":"The author's attitude towards limiting screens in schools is _____.","options":[{"key":"A","text":"indifferent, because technology is thought to have little effect on classroom focus"},{"key":"B","text":"strongly opposed, since all digital devices are regarded as essential teaching tools"},{"key":"C","text":"uncritically enthusiastic, because bans are assumed to raise test scores immediately"},{"key":"D","text":"cautiously supportive, because evidence links screens to distraction while careful use may help"}],"answer":"D","kicker":"[D]","analysis":["【题型】作者态度题·政策态度识别","【难度】⭐⭐⭐⭐","【定位】Paragraph 5","【解析】","- 原文（Para.5）：The evidence is growing that tablets, mobile phones and other such screens are distracting youngsters from their studies and diverting them from hobbies, like reading books, that instil the ability to focus. So limits on screens seem prudent.","- 原文（Para.5）：Used carefully, technology can benefit classrooms. More usually, snazzy new tools and devices just get in the way.","分析：","- 选项A（错误）：态度偷换陷阱。作者认为 evidence is growing，并非漠不关心。","- 选项B（错误）：反向态度陷阱。作者支持更多国家限制手机，而不是强烈反对。","- 选项C（错误）：程度过度陷阱。原文只说 seem prudent，没有承诺立即提高成绩。","- 选项D（正确）：limits on screens seem prudent 表示谨慎支持；同时 Used carefully, technology can benefit classrooms 保留技术合理使用的可能性。","【命题技巧】","- 态度形容词匹配：prudent 对应 cautiously supportive。","- 让步语气识别：Used carefully 表明作者不主张一刀切排斥技术。"]},{"number":25,"stem":"Which of the following would be the best title for the text?","options":[{"key":"A","text":"AI to the Rescue: Why Machines Can Replace Human Learning"},{"key":"B","text":"No More Excuses: Rich Countries Must Reverse Falling School Scores"},{"key":"C","text":"Culture Wars in Classrooms: Why Exams Should Be Abolished"},{"key":"D","text":"Britain First: The Only Successful Model for School Reform"}],"answer":"B","kicker":"[B]","analysis":["【题型】主旨大意题·最佳标题","【难度】⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐","【定位】Full text","【解析】","- 原文（Para.1）：Schools in RICH countries are failing... A slow-motion disaster is under way.","- 原文（Para.6）：An even bigger problem, though, is muddle-headed thinking among educators.","- 原文（Para.9）：Falling scores will not be fixed in a jiffy... Today’s leaders seem to have cooled on schools, except as culture-war battlegrounds. That lack of serious attention invites calamity.","分析：","- 选项A（错误）：反向主旨陷阱。作者专门反驳把思考外包给 AI 的观点。","- 选项B（正确）：No more excuses 呼应文章标题和论证核心；全文从成绩下滑、后果、错误归因、教育问题和改革难度一路强调富裕国家必须严肃应对。","- 选项C（错误）：细节扩大陷阱。culture-war battlegrounds 只出现在末段，且作者并不主张废除考试。","- 选项D（错误）：以偏概全陷阱。英国只是避免下滑并取得进步的例子，不是全文唯一模型。","【命题技巧】","- 问题-原因-对策整合：成绩下滑、AI 误解、屏幕、教育理念和改革滞后构成全文链条。","- 标题语气校准：正确标题保留紧迫感和政策呼吁，不引入反向主张。","— — — — — — — — — —","本文档由我们自研的考研英语阅读理解大模型生成。该模型基于近10年考研英语真题进行训练。我们会根据同学们的反馈持续优化模型能力。","本文档为【福利】，不提供答疑服务，欢迎大家自行交流使用。","【两个常见问题】","随着AI技术的不断进步，通过知识投喂和持续训练，大模型已逐渐具备模仿真题命题风格的能力。这份文档正是这一尝试的成果。它仅作为福利提供给大家，起到辅助检验的作用。你可以根据自身需要选择使用，如果有所顾虑，完全可以不用。","如果对答案解析有疑惑，直接打开任何AI大模型（如Deepseek、Kimi等），上传该篇原文文档，然后复制粘贴对应的题目和选项，说清楚你的疑问，大模型会回应解释对应的问题。"]}]},{"file":"【福利】9.17检验题__完型填空(1).docx","type":"cloze","instructions":["Directions: Read the following text. Choose the best word(s) for each numbered blank and mark A, B, C or D on the ANSWER SHEET. (10 points)"],"passage":["This argument could not be more wrong. AI will not make it pointless to train human brains, any more than the invention of cars eliminated the need for physical exercise. On the contrary, adapting to a world of rapid, AI-driven change will require more mental ____【11】____, not less. And precisely because AI will increase the ____【12】____ for people to outsource difficult cognitive tasks, schools have an extra ____【13】____ to help pupils exercise their brains and acquire the hard habits of thinking. Failure to ____【14】____ them with the basic building blocks of a well-developed mind, such as literacy and numeracy, is ____【15】____.","PISA scores began drifting downwards around 2012. The evidence is growing that tablets, mobile phones and other such screens are ____【16】____ youngsters from their studies and diverting them from hobbies, like reading books, that instil the ability to ____【17】____. So limits on screens seem ____【18】____. About half of countries now ban mobile phones from schools to some extent; more should ____【19】____. Used carefully, technology can benefit classrooms. More usually, snazzy new tools and devices just get in the ____【20】____."],"items":[{"number":11,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"flexibility"},{"key":"B","text":"stability"},{"key":"C","text":"agility"},{"key":"D","text":"endurance"}],"answer":"C","kicker":"C (agility)","analysis":["【题型】名词辨析·认知语义","【难度】⭐⭐","【定位】Paragraph 1, 第3句","【解析】","[A] flexibility（灵活性）：可通，但 mental agility 更强调快速适应 AI 变化。近义干扰。","[B] stability（稳定性）：与 rapid, AI-driven change 的动态语境不合。逻辑方向干扰。","[C] agility（敏捷、机敏）：mental agility 指思维敏捷，适应快速变化需要更多思维敏捷。正确答案。","[D] endurance（耐力）：偏体力或长期坚持，不精确对应快速应变。语义场干扰。","— — — — — — — — — — —"]},{"number":12,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"temptation"},{"key":"B","text":"obligation"},{"key":"C","text":"capacity"},{"key":"D","text":"reluctance"}],"answer":"A","kicker":"A (temptation)","analysis":["【题型】名词辨析·心理诱因","【难度】⭐⭐","【定位】Paragraph 1, 第4句","【解析】","[A] temptation（诱惑）：increase the temptation to outsource... 指增加外包认知任务的诱惑。正确答案。","[B] obligation（义务）：外包思考不是义务。逻辑方向干扰。","[C] capacity（能力）：能力增加不必然产生负面诱惑。语义场干扰。","[D] reluctance（不情愿）：方向相反。逻辑方向干扰。","— — — — — — — — — — —"]},{"number":13,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"budget"},{"key":"B","text":"reason"},{"key":"C","text":"method"},{"key":"D","text":"duty"}],"answer":"D","kicker":"D (duty)","analysis":["【题型】名词辨析·责任语义","【难度】⭐⭐","【定位】Paragraph 1, 第4句","【解析】","[A] budget（预算）：后文不是经费问题。语义场干扰。","[B] reason（理由）：schools have a reason 语义较弱，不能体现教育责任。程度差异干扰。","[C] method（方法）：不是具体方法。语义场干扰。","[D] duty（责任）：an extra duty to help pupils... 指学校额外承担的帮助学生的责任。正确答案。","— — — — — — — — — — —"]},{"number":14,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"furnish"},{"key":"B","text":"compare"},{"key":"C","text":"burden"},{"key":"D","text":"deprive"}],"answer":"A","kicker":"A (furnish)","analysis":["【题型】动词辨析·供给搭配","【难度】⭐⭐⭐","【定位】Paragraph 1, 第5句","【解析】","[A] furnish（提供、装备）：furnish them with the basic building blocks 指为学生提供基础思维能力。正确答案。","[B] compare（比较）：不与 with the basic building blocks 形成恰当供给义。搭配干扰。","[C] burden（使负担）：语义方向相反。逻辑方向干扰。","[D] deprive（剥夺）：deprive 通常搭配 of，且方向相反。搭配干扰。","— — — — — — — — — — —"]},{"number":15,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"unavoidable"},{"key":"B","text":"understandable"},{"key":"C","text":"inexcusable"},{"key":"D","text":"controversial"}],"answer":"C","kicker":"C (inexcusable)","analysis":["【题型】形容词辨析·态度强度","【难度】⭐⭐","【定位】Paragraph 1, 第5句","【解析】","[A] unavoidable（不可避免的）：作者认为失败不可原谅，不是无法避免。逻辑方向干扰。","[B] understandable（可以理解的）：语气相反。感情色彩干扰。","[C] inexcusable（不可原谅的）：is inexcusable 表达作者强烈批评。正确答案。","[D] controversial（有争议的）：作者态度明确，不认为 merely controversial。程度差异干扰。","— — — — — — — — — — —"]},{"number":16,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"liberating"},{"key":"B","text":"distracting"},{"key":"C","text":"separating"},{"key":"D","text":"recruiting"}],"answer":"B","kicker":"B (distracting)","analysis":["【题型】动词辨析·现在分词语义","【难度】⭐⭐","【定位】Paragraph 2, 第2句","【解析】","[A] liberating（解放）：与 diverting away from studies 的负面效果矛盾。逻辑方向干扰。","[B] distracting（使分心）：screens are distracting youngsters from their studies 指屏幕使学生分心。正确答案。","[C] separating（分离）：可搭 from，但未强调注意力被引开。近义干扰。","[D] recruiting（招募）：语义无关。语义场干扰。","— — — — — — — — — — —"]},{"number":17,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"memorise"},{"key":"B","text":"compete"},{"key":"C","text":"rest"},{"key":"D","text":"focus"}],"answer":"D","kicker":"D (focus)","analysis":["【题型】动词辨析·能力语义","【难度】⭐⭐","【定位】Paragraph 2, 第2句","【解析】","[A] memorise（记忆）：阅读习惯培养的是专注能力。语义场干扰。","[B] compete（竞争）：非本段主题。语义场干扰。","[C] rest（休息）：方向相反。逻辑方向干扰。","[D] focus（专注）：阅读等爱好培养的是专注能力。正确答案。","— — — — — — — — — — —"]},{"number":18,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"prudent"},{"key":"B","text":"radical"},{"key":"C","text":"futile"},{"key":"D","text":"optional"}],"answer":"A","kicker":"A (prudent)","analysis":["【题型】形容词辨析·评价语义","【难度】⭐⭐⭐","【定位】Paragraph 2, 第3句","【解析】","[A] prudent（谨慎的、合理的）：limits on screens seem prudent 指限制屏幕似乎稳妥。正确答案。","[B] radical（激进的）：证据逐渐增强，措施并不极端。程度差异干扰。","[C] futile（徒劳的）：作者支持限制，不是认为无效。逻辑方向干扰。","[D] optional（可选的）：语气过弱。程度差异干扰。","— — — — — — — — — — —"]},{"number":19,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"hesitate"},{"key":"B","text":"debate"},{"key":"C","text":"follow"},{"key":"D","text":"wait"}],"answer":"C","kicker":"C (follow)","analysis":["【题型】动词辨析·建议动作","【难度】⭐⭐","【定位】Paragraph 2, 第4句","【解析】","[A] hesitate（犹豫）：与 should 的建议方向不合。逻辑方向干扰。","[B] debate（辩论）：证据已支持采取行动。程度差异干扰。","[C] follow（跟随）：more should follow 指更多国家应效仿。正确答案。","[D] wait（等待）：与 should 的行动建议相反。逻辑方向干扰。","— — — — — — — — — — —"]},{"number":20,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"course"},{"key":"B","text":"way"},{"key":"C","text":"hands"},{"key":"D","text":"light"}],"answer":"B","kicker":"B (way)","analysis":["【题型】名词辨析·固定搭配","【难度】⭐⭐","【定位】Paragraph 2, 第6句","【解析】","[A] course（课程）：course 不用于 get in the course。搭配干扰。","[B] way（道路）：get in the way 为固定搭配，意为“妨碍”。正确答案。","[C] hands（手）：get in the hands 多指“被使用”，不合语境。搭配干扰。","[D] light（光）：in the light of 指“鉴于”，与 just get in 搭配不当。形近干扰。","— — — — — — — — — —","本文档由我们自研的考研英语完型填空大模型生成。该模型基于近10年考研英语真题进行训练。我们会根据同学们的反馈持续优化模型能力。","本文档为【福利】，不提供答疑服务，欢迎大家自行交流使用。","【两个常见问题】","随着AI技术的不断进步，通过知识投喂和持续训练，大模型已逐渐具备模仿真题命题风格的能力。这份文档正是这一尝试的成果。它仅作为福利提供给大家，起到辅助检验的作用。你可以根据自身需要选择使用，如果有所顾虑，完全可以不用。"]}]},{"file":"【福利】9.17检验题__新题型__7选5(1).docx","type":"gap-fill","instructions":["Directions:"],"passage":["Part B","In the following text, some sentences have been removed. For Questions 41-45, choose the most suitable one from the list A-G to fit into each of the numbered blanks. There are two extra choices, which do not fit in any of the blanks. Mark your answers on the ANSWER SHEET. (10 points)","Schools in rich countries are failing. The latest scores on PISA tests of reading, maths and science, released on September 8th, show a decline that has continued since the tests, administered by the OECD, began in 2000. Educators can no longer blame it all on covid-19 lockdowns, which badly messed up lessons and caused PISA grades to drop. The pandemic is now over, but scores have continued to fall roughly as fast. In 35 rich countries that take part in the tests, a typical 15-year-old now reads no better than a 14-year-old did a decade ago. (41)","Test scores are not the full measure of a child. But they are a good predictor of desirable outcomes and a bulwark against many of the worst ones. Kids who do well on tests tend to earn more, live longer and healthier lives, and commit fewer crimes. By one estimate, pushing up PISA scores by a mere quarter of a standard deviation might increase annual growth in rich countries by half a percentage point.","(42) The adults of the future will be able to outsource their thinking to clever machines, just as they use machines to perform innumerable physical tasks. So why sweat about swotting?","(43) AI will not make it pointless to train human brains, any more than the invention of cars eliminated the need for physical exercise. On the contrary, adapting to a world of rapid, AI-driven change will require more mental agility, not less. And precisely because AI will increase the temptation for people to outsource difficult cognitive tasks, schools have an extra duty to help pupils exercise their brains and acquire the hard habits of thinking.","PISA scores began drifting downwards around 2012. The evidence is growing that tablets, mobile phones and other such screens are distracting youngsters from their studies and diverting them from hobbies, like reading books, that instil the ability to focus. (44) About half of countries now ban mobile phones from schools to some extent; more should follow. Used carefully, technology can benefit classrooms. More usually, snazzy new tools and devices just get in the way.","An even bigger problem, though, is muddle-headed thinking among educators. Some have fallen for the leftist canard that tests are harmful and grades are racist. Some have allowed reasonable worries about children’s mental health to warp into an excuse for low expectations. Some unhelpful habits acquired during the pandemic have stuck: children are still missing more lessons than before, and many teachers who relaxed their grading standards have failed to tighten up again.","What to do? There is little evidence that shovelling more cash into schools will make much difference: Japan spends 30% less per pupil than America and gets much better results. But research needs a big boost. Teaching, as a profession, remains shamefully uninterested in the evidence about which techniques work best. Education departments in universities contain some of the weakest and most radical staff, often keener on abstract ideas of social justice than the more concrete benefits that better grades might bring to pupils.","(45) Britain is one. In 2009 its pupils scraped into the world’s top 30 in maths and reading. Now they sit in the top ten. Some of this stems from a return in England, under a previous Conservative government, to old-fashioned things such as rigorous exams, tough inspections and fact-filled curriculums.","Falling scores will not be fixed in a jiffy. Because it takes time to educate a child, the latest test results bear the marks of decisions taken ten to 20 years ago. That long lag makes reform politically harder. Governments that make schools better will seldom see benefits they can boast about before the next election. But they should care anyway. Politicians in past decades, from Tony Blair to George Bush, have won office promising to fix education. Today’s leaders seem to have cooled on schools, except as culture-war battlegrounds. That lack of serious attention invites calamity. As machines grow brighter, don’t let teenagers grow any dimmer.","[A] Teachers should therefore relax grading rules until children’s mental health improves.","[B] Limiting screens is therefore a prudent first step.","[C] Some countries show that a return to rigorous routines can reverse the slide.","[D] A tempting reply is that clever machines will soon make human thinking less necessary.","[E] The pandemic explanation no longer covers a continuing, slow-moving collapse.","[F] This seductive argument is profoundly wrong.","[G] The quickest remedy would be to increase schools’ core funding."],"items":[{"number":41,"stem":"","options":[],"answer":"E","kicker":"【答案】E","analysis":["【定位】第一段段末；空格前写成绩继续下降，第二段转入成绩的意义。","【解析】前文先否定疫情解释，再以“现在仍快速下降”推进；【E】中的“pandemic explanation”和“continuing, slow-moving collapse”把两层信息收束为段末结论。第二段转而讨论成绩的重要性，也与危机判断自然衔接。","逐项分析"]},{"number":42,"stem":"","options":[],"answer":"D","kicker":"【答案】D","analysis":["【定位】第三段段首；空格后以“The adults of the future…”展开。","【解析】【D】中的“tempting reply”开启新的反问立场；“clever machines”和“human thinking”直接对应后文的“outsource their thinking”和“clever machines”。它使第三段成为被反驳的对象句。","逐项分析"]},{"number":43,"stem":"","options":[],"answer":"F","kicker":"【答案】F","analysis":["【定位】第四段段首；空格前提出 AI 诱惑，空格后开始反驳。","【解析】【F】的“This seductive argument”唯一稳定地回指【D】提出的诱惑性观点；“profoundly wrong”开启后文“could not be more wrong”式的反驳，并引出 AI 反而要求更多思维训练的论证。","逐项分析"]},{"number":44,"stem":"","options":[],"answer":"B","kicker":"【答案】B","analysis":["【定位】第五段段中；空格前写屏幕分散注意力，空格后写多国禁止手机。","【解析】前句给出 screens 干扰学习和阅读的证据，并出现“distraction”和“focus”的因果链。【B】的“Limiting screens”承接该证据，“therefore a prudent first step”又被后文“ban mobile phones; more should follow”具体化。","逐项分析"]},{"number":45,"stem":"","options":[],"answer":"C","kicker":"【答案】C","analysis":["【定位】第八段段首；空格后以“Britain is one”举例。","【解析】【C】先说“some countries”能通过严格常规逆转下滑，后句“Britain is one”立即给出实例；随后 rigorous exams、tough inspections 和 fact-filled curriculums 正是“rigorous routines”的具体化。","逐项分析","干扰项设置","本题主要使用逻辑反转、措施错位和话题回跳。【A】【G】利用教育改革的常见思路设置措施陷阱，但方向与原文相反；【D】【F】构成一组“诱因—反驳”，若单独放入空格会造成回指断裂；【E】利用首段关键词诱导回跳，但无法服务后文。","— — — — — — — — — —","本文档由我们自研的考研英语新题型大模型生成。该模型基于近10年考研英语真题进行训练。我们会根据同学们的反馈持续优化模型能力。目前是在测试阶段，该题型仅提供到12月底。","本文档为【福利】，不提供答疑服务，欢迎大家自行交流使用。","【两个常见问题】","随着AI技术的不断进步，通过知识投喂和持续训练，大模型已逐渐具备模仿真题命题风格的能力。这份文档正是这一尝试的成果。它仅作为福利提供给大家，起到辅助检验的作用。你可以根据自身需要选择使用，如果有所顾虑，完全可以不用。"]}]}],"qa":[],"originals":[{"kind":"reading","label":"精读","file":"9.17 精读(1).pdf","ext":".pdf","bytes":1257911,"date":"2026-09-17","range":"","title":"No more excuses","sourcePath":"D:\\外刊\\9.17 精读(1).pdf","raw":".cache\\periodical-raw2"},{"kind":"test","label":"检验题","file":"【福利】9.17 检验题__阅读理解(1).docx","ext":".docx","bytes":54966,"date":"2026-09-17","range":"","title":"","sourcePath":"D:\\外刊\\【福利】9.17 检验题__阅读理解(1).docx","raw":".cache\\periodical-raw2"},{"kind":"test","label":"检验题","file":"【福利】9.17检验题__完型填空(1).docx","ext":".docx","bytes":58216,"date":"2026-09-17","range":"","title":"","sourcePath":"D:\\外刊\\【福利】9.17检验题__完型填空(1).docx","raw":".cache\\periodical-raw2"},{"kind":"test","label":"检验题","file":"【福利】9.17检验题__新题型__7选5(1).docx","ext":".docx","bytes":78644,"date":"2026-09-17","range":"","title":"","sourcePath":"D:\\外刊\\【福利】9.17检验题__新题型__7选5(1).docx","raw":".cache\\periodical-raw2"},{"kind":"layout","label":"杂志排版","file":"9.17杂志排版(1).pdf","ext":".pdf","bytes":682215,"date":"2026-09-17","range":"","title":"","sourcePath":"D:\\外刊\\9.17杂志排版(1).pdf","raw":".cache\\periodical-raw2"}]};
